{25}{4},\\,y\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeef9da7-ffa6-5021-aee7-5cc4d1113afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{25}{4},\\,y\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280684} :answer-field/id #uuid "43190c17-eebf-5453-8891-2414e96f88c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280947}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2dc3a2b7-620d-5940-a1aa-2a26502f43cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-16200" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5t-{t}^{2},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5t-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-({t}^{2}-5t) \\\\  & =-{(t-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(t-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${(t-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}\\ge 0,$ we can conclude that $x\\le \\frac{25}{4}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that ${t}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Consequently, $y\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x\\le \\frac{25}{4}$ and $y\\ge 0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8605aaa0-1b98-56eb-a570-a43a2a70d923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8605aaa0-1b98-56eb-a570-a43a2a70d923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291204} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a6ce4b8-3146-5a15-914e-728e1ea47524" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291775} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid